course: dictionary
lesson: 14064
title: Matchstick
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A matchstick is the thin wooden stick of a match. People strike the tip to light a fire or a candle. «Grandma lit the candles with a matchstick.» Here matchstick means the real stick you hold.
explain: Matchstick can also describe something very thin. «The little robot had matchstick arms and legs.» The robot's arms are not made of wood. They are so thin that they look like matchsticks. «Ruby drew matchstick men with long, thin bodies.» The clues show the word means thin.
explain: Matchsticks are useful in maths and patterns too. «Move one matchstick to make the sum correct.» You count and move the sticks to solve the puzzle. Always check the clues around the word to see which meaning fits.
fact: A matchstick is the thin wooden stick of a match.
fact: Matches are for adults to use, so never play with them.
fact: «Matchstick» can describe something very thin, like «matchstick arms».
fact: Matchsticks are used in craft, such as building models.
fact: Matchstick puzzles ask you to move or count sticks to make a shape or a sum.
exercise: «Grandma lit the candle with a matchstick.» What is a matchstick? | *A thin wooden stick used to light fires | A large metal box for storing food | A small wooden cup | A bright light that never goes out
exercise: «The little robot had matchstick arms.» What does matchstick mean here? | Very strong | *Very thin | Made of shiny metal | Very short
exercise: «Move one matchstick to make the sum correct.» What are the matchsticks used for in this sentence? | Drawing a picture of a house | Lighting a fire | *Solving a maths puzzle | Cooking dinner
test: «Ben built a bridge from matchsticks for his science project.» What did Ben build from matchsticks? | A kite | *A bridge | A tower of bricks | A boat
test: Which is the correct safety rule about matches? | Keep them in your pocket to show your friends | Give them to small children to hold | *Never play with them; they are for adults | Play with them outdoors when no one is watching
exam: «Ruby drew matchstick men with long, thin bodies.» What does matchstick describe here? | *Very thin | Very fat | Very tall | Very bright
